xists):** 1010's out-neighbours are {158, 189, 1736} (step_03); 883's in-neighbours are {639, 704, 1038, 1105, 1209, 1704, 1786, 1789} (step_04). Two 3-hop static routes exist: 1010\u2192189\u2192704\u2192883 (steps 11\u201313) and 1010\u2192158\u2192{639,704}\u2192883 (steps 14, 18, 19, 23). There is **no** direct 1010\u2192883 edge (step_21).\n\n**Confounder controls:** the candidate relays are exactly the graph's hubs \u2014 158 is the most active node (1029 events, 626 partners) and 639 is third (486 events, 353 partners) (steps 15, 16), with thousands of incident triangles (steps 17, 27). So a static path through them is nearly guaranteed by hub popularity alone and carries almost no cascade-specific information. Meanwhile the endpoint edges are single, non-repeating events (1 event each, steps 11\u201313, 19), and 1010 itself fires only 6 times across the whole 100k horizon (step_02).\n\n**Temporal evidence (missing):** every available tool aggregates over the full horizon [0, 100000] (steps 01\u201324); none returns event timestamps or restricts to [51317, 89717]. I therefore cannot verify (a) that any 1010 event falls inside the query window, (b) that the hops occur in increasing time order, or (c) that consecutive hops are within \u0394 = 600. Rough base rates show this cannot be assumed: the window covers only 38.4% of the horizon (step_20), and a randomly-timed 2-relay chain satisfying both \u0394 \u2264 600 gaps has probability ~3\u00d710\u207b\u2074 (step_26) \u2014 i.e., the static path is far more likely to be a hub artifact than a cascade, but ruling it out would still require the timestamps I cannot access.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_20982933\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1010, 883, 189, 704, 158, 639],\n  \"window\": [51317, 89717],\n  \"confidence\": 0.7,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"}